---
import Layout from '../../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
const pageTitle = "Likutay Halachos - Hilchos Gairim Aleph";
const pageDescription = "Breslov teachings from Likutay Halachos: Hilchos Gairim Aleph";
---
<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <div class="page-banner">
    <h1>Likutay Halachos</h1>
    <p>Yoreh Daya - 2 - Hilchos Gairim Aleph</p>
  </div>
<style>
  @import url('https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=EB+Garamond:ital,wght@0,400;0,500;0,600;0,700;1,400;1,500&family=Cinzel:wght@400;600;700&family=Noto+Serif+Hebrew&display=swap');
  .lh-container { --ink:#1c1c2e;--parchment:#f7f3ea;--parchment2:#eee8d5;--gold:#a07830;--gold-light:#c8a050;--sapphire:#1e3a5f;--sapphire2:#2e5580;--sapphire-pale:#dde8f5;--ruby:#7a1a2e;--border:#b89860;--muted:#5a5040;--verse-color:#1e3a5f;--source-color:#888070; font-family:'EB Garamond',Georgia,serif; font-size:18px; line-height:1.85; color:var(--ink); background:var(--parchment); text-align:justify; max-width:800px; margin:0 auto; padding:40px 20px 80px; }
  .lh-container .book-title { text-align: center; margin-bottom: 50px; padding-bottom: 30px; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border); }
  .lh-container .book-title h1 { font-family: 'Cinzel', serif; font-size: 2.2em; font-weight: 700; color: var(--sapphire); letter-spacing: 0.03em; margin-bottom: 4px; }
  .lh-container .section-header { text-align: center; margin: 50px 0 30px; padding: 15px 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--border); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .lh-container .halacha-header { text-align: center; margin: 55px 0 20px; padding: 20px 0; border-top: 2px solid var(--gold); border-bottom: 2px solid var(--gold); }
  .lh-container .sa-excerpt { text-align: center; margin: 20px 0 35px; padding: 15px 25px; background: var(--parchment2); border-left: 3px solid var(--gold); border-right: 3px solid var(--gold); font-style: italic; }
  .lh-container .ois { font-weight: 700; font-size: 1.15em; color: var(--gold); margin-top: 35px; margin-bottom: 10px; display: block; }
  .lh-container p { margin-bottom: 18px; text-indent: 1.5em; }
  .lh-container p:first-of-type, .lh-container .ois + p, .lh-container .sa-excerpt + p { text-indent: 0; }
  .lh-container .heb { font-style: italic; color: var(--ruby); }
  .page-banner { background: linear-gradient(135deg, #1a365d 0%, #2c5282 100%); color: white; padding: 3rem 2rem; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 0; }
  .page-banner h1 { font-family: 'Cinzel', serif; font-size: 2.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; color: white; }
</style>
  <div class="lh-container">

<div class="page">

  <div class="series-label">Likutay Halachos &mdash; Yoreh Day&lsquo;ah</div>
  <h1>Hilchos Gairim <span style="font-weight:normal;font-size:17px;">(Laws of Converts)</span></h1>
  <h2>Halachah 1</h2>
  <hr class="rule">

  <p>
    The <span class="key-term">ger</span> [convert] enters the faith of Israel through milah
    and immersion, and requires a <span class="key-term">bais din</span> [court of three], etc.
    For originally he was a gentile from the seventy nations, who are gripped by the
    <span class="key-term">ra ha-kolel</span> [the inclusive evil], which is the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">orlah</span>, as is cited in the words of Rabbeinu
    <span class="editorial-note">((in Siman 19))</span>. And therefore when he comes to enter
    the faith of Israel, he must be circumcised &mdash; and specifically through this mitzvah
    does he enter the faith of Israel, for it is the <span class="key-term">tikun ha-k&rsquo;lali</span>
    [the general remedy]: through subduing the <span class="key-term">orlah</span>, thereby the
    inclusive evil that had gripped him among the seventy nations is subdued, and he enters the
    faith of Israel. For this is the primary holiness of Israel &mdash; the guarding of the
    <span class="key-term">bris</span>, which is the aspect of milah, through which
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;He has exalted us above every tongue&rdquo;</em></span>,
    as cited in the words of Rabbeinu, may his light shine <span class="editorial-note">((ibid.))</span>.
  </p>

  <p>
    And afterwards he is obligated in immersion, so that he become a new creation
    (<span class="key-term">b&rsquo;riyah chadashah</span>). For immersion is the aspect of
    becoming hidden within the waters of the <span class="key-term">mikveh</span>, which is the
    aspect of the <span class="key-term">olam ha-ba</span> [the World to Come], where one is
    renewed and becomes like a new creation &mdash; in the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">teshuvah</span>, the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;Return us, O God&hellip; renew our days&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Eichah 5:21]</span>. And all this was explained above in
    <span class="key-term">Hilchos T&rsquo;vilas Kailim</span>
    <span class="editorial-note">((Halachah 1))</span> and in
    <span class="key-term">Hilchos M&rsquo;gilah</span>
    <span class="editorial-note">((Halachah 1))</span>. And therefore the convert is obligated in
    immersion, so that through this he truly be renewed and transformed from a body of the
    seventy nations into a body of Israel, as above.
  </p>

  <p>
    And a <span class="key-term">bais din</span> is required &mdash; and while he stands in the
    water, the <span class="key-term">bais din</span> informs him of some of the commandments,
    and he accepts upon himself the yoke of Heaven, etc. For the primary holiness of Israel is
    the aspect of the <span class="key-term">n&rsquo;kudah</span> [the inner point] that exists
    within every individual of Israel, which is the aspect of his <span class="key-term">neshamah</span>
    [soul]. And all the <span class="key-term">n&rsquo;kudos</span> of Israel receive from the
    <span class="key-term">n&rsquo;kudah ha-k&rsquo;lalis</span> [the universal inclusive point],
    as cited in the teaching that begins <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;v&rsquo;atem tih&rsquo;yu
    li&hellip;&rdquo;</em></span> <span class="editorial-note">((in Siman 34))</span>.
  </p>

  <p>
    And it was already explained above in <span class="key-term">Hilchos N&rsquo;darim</span>
    <span class="editorial-note">((Halachah 1))</span> that a court of three is in the aspect
    of <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;tzaddik moshel&rdquo;</em></span>
    [&ldquo;the righteous one rules&rdquo;], the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;Yisrael mamsh&rsquo;losav&rdquo;</em></span>
    [&ldquo;Israel, his dominions&rdquo;] &mdash; for they have the power to rule and adjudicate
    as they determine. And they are in the aspect of the <span class="key-term">n&rsquo;kudah
    ha-k&rsquo;lalis</span>, for they comprise all of Israel: Kohen, Levi, and Yisrael, which
    is the aspect of <span class="key-term">k&rsquo;lal Yisrael</span> that is the aspect of
    the <span class="key-term">n&rsquo;kudah ha-k&rsquo;lalis</span>. And therefore the convert
    requires a <span class="key-term">bais din</span> so that they illuminate within him from
    their <span class="key-term">n&rsquo;kudah</span>, which is the aspect of the
    <span class="key-term">n&rsquo;kudah ha-k&rsquo;lalis</span>, so that the convert receive
    the illumination of the <span class="key-term">n&rsquo;kudah</span> &mdash; through which he
    merits the holiness of Israel, whose essence depends upon the
    <span class="key-term">n&rsquo;kudah</span>, as above. For while he stands in the water they
    inform him of the commandments, and through this &mdash; that they speak the commandments
    with their mouths &mdash; they illuminate within him the <span class="key-term">n&rsquo;kudah</span>,
    which is in the aspect of <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;my mouth speaks wisdom&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Tehillim 49:4]</span> &mdash; and through this he receives the
    <span class="key-term">n&rsquo;kudah</span> and then becomes Israel.
  </p>

  <p>
    And therefore specifically while standing in the water they inform him, for it is stated
    that the soul (<span class="key-term">neshamah</span>) cannot return except through immersion
    &mdash; and therefore when he is in the water, when he must receive the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">neshamah</span>, then they inform him and illuminate within him the
    illumination of the <span class="key-term">n&rsquo;kudah</span> as above, and he receives
    the <span class="key-term">neshamah</span> there through the immersion, for the
    <span class="key-term">n&rsquo;kudah</span> is the <span class="key-term">neshamah</span>,
    as above.
  </p>

  <p>
    And therefore the milah must precede the immersion, for it is impossible to receive the
    illumination of the <span class="key-term">n&rsquo;kudah</span> except through milah. For
    the <span class="key-term">n&rsquo;kudah</span> dwells in the place of
    <span class="key-term">bris shalom</span> [the covenant of peace], as cited in the teaching
    <em>&ldquo;v&rsquo;atem tih&rsquo;yu li&hellip;&rdquo;</em>, see there. For the
    <span class="key-term">orlah</span> is in the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">cherpah</span> [disgrace], in the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;for it is a disgrace to us&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Yehoshua 5:9]</span>, which is the aspect of
    <span class="verse-quote"><em>&ldquo;disgrace has broken my heart&rdquo;</em></span>
    <span class="verse-source">[Tehillim 69:21]</span> &mdash; which is the aspect of
    blemishing the <span class="key-term">n&rsquo;kudah</span>, see there. And therefore
    originally, when he was a gentile, he was uncircumcised, for all nations are uncircumcised,
    as explained in the Mishnah. For the sustenance of the seventy nations is from the
    <span class="key-term">sh&rsquo;viras ha-kailim</span> [the shattering of the vessels],
    from which the grip of the <span class="key-term">k&rsquo;lipos</span> [husks/shells] draws,
    which is the aspect of <span class="key-term">orlah</span>, the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">cherpah</span>, as above. And therefore when he wishes to enter the
    faith of Israel he requires milah to remove the disgrace, to take him out from the aspect
    of <span class="key-term">sh&rsquo;virah</span> [shattering], and the aspect of
    <span class="key-term">bris shalom</span> is revealed &mdash; where the light of the
    <span class="key-term">n&rsquo;kudah</span> dwells among Israel. And then he receives the
    light of the <span class="key-term">n&rsquo;kudah</span> from the court of three Israelites
    while standing in the water, as above. And then he is truly renewed, as above &mdash; that is,
    he goes out from the aspect of <span class="key-term">sh&rsquo;virah</span> to the aspect
    of <span class="key-term">tikun</span> [rectification], which is the aspect of a truly new
    creation (<span class="key-term">b&rsquo;riyah chadashah mamash</span>), as is known.
  </p>

  <div class="cross-ref">
    <strong>Cross-reference:</strong> Hilchos Gairim, Halachah 2 &mdash; in Hilchos Avadim,
    Halachah 2, os tes.
  </div>

</div>

  </div>
</Layout>